ict,\n        \"name_mapping\": names,\n        \"total\": total_allocated\n    }\n```\n\nNow we need to put this into a JSON string. We'll escape: newlines become \\n, backslashes become \\\\, double quotes become \\\". Also the indentation spaces should be preserved. We'll produce a single line string with \\n for newline.\n\nWe'll output: {\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    segments = case['segments']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    ... \"}\n\nNeed to ensure string itself is valid JSON. Let's produce manually.\n\nThe source code contains single quotes, which are fine inside JSON double-quoted string. We need to escape any double quotes inside the source (there are none). Also escape backslashes? There are no backslashes. Newlines become \\n.\n\nI'll write the code string with explicit \\n.\n\nBut to avoid mistakes, I'll write the JSON directly in the response.\n\nFinal answer should be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". So:\n\n{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    segments = case['segments']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    total_min = sum(s['minimum'] for s in segments)\\n    total_cap = sum(s['capacity'] for s in segments)\\n    if budget < total_min or budget > total_cap:\\n        return {'error': 'budget out of range'}\\n    allocations = [s['minimum'] for s in segments]\\n    remaining = budget - total_min\\n    while remaining > 0:\\n        best_index = -1\\n        best_score = -1.0\\n        for i, seg in enumerate(segments):\\n            if allocations[i] >= seg['capacity']:\\n                continue\\n            score = seg['importance'] / (allocations[i] + 1)\\n            if score > best_score:\\n                best_score = score\\n                best_index = i\\n        if best_index == -1:\\n            break\\n        allocations[best_index] += 1\\n        remaining -= 1\\n    names = [s['name'] for s in segments]\\n    alloc_dict = {names[i]: allocations[i] for i in range(len(segments))}\\n    total_